        <body>
            <div xml:id="d405">

                <pb facs="0741" n="645" sameAs="frus:frus1874/pg645"/>

                <head>No. 403.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Davis to Mr. De
                    Long.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1873-08-21"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 21,
                        1873</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 212.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your No. 439, of the 5th
                    ultimo, in which inquiry is made as to whether a citizen of the United States
                    residing in Japan is at liberty to surrender to that government his privileges
                    of extraterritoriality, I have to state in answer that, as an American, he
                    cannot alter treaties existing between the two countries, but he can expatriate
                    himself if he wishes so to do.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>J. C. B. DAVIS,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                        Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
